PUBLIC RESPONSE: O – Yes for SSE, No for Nasdaq, No for HKEX; Special trading rules and regulations.

With the signed definition buyer15s = p′/p –1 and seller15s = –(p′/p –1) and p′ the 15s later mid, execution at/near ask/bid gives mechanically E[buyer15s]≈–½spread <0< +½spread≈E[seller15s] under zero drift. An observed E[buyer15s] > E[seller15s] on SZSE therefore requires trade-sign drift on the 15s horizon large enough to overcome the ~½ spread penalty for buys and the ~½ spread benefit for sells.

That drift is a mainland A-share regime artefact, not a universal microstructure law.

SZSE and SSE share the same regime:
* T+1 delivery, no same-day reversal of a long, so sellers are mechanically pre-existing longs;
* ±10% daily price limits with a closing call auction;
* de-facto short-sale prohibition / severe short-sale constraints for the retail dominated universe;
* sell-side only 0.1% ad-valorem stamp duty;
* retail dominated order flow.

With short selling essentially unavailable negative information cannot be expressed on the sell side. The supply side is mechanically constrained and a buy market order can lift the ask and create a persistent order-book imbalance that lasts tens of seconds because T+1, price limits and short-sale constraints blunt arbitrage. Sell-initiated flow is liquidity/distress/rebalancing/profit-taking dominated, its impact is muted and reverts faster. Hence E[p′/p –1|buy] can be >0 and larger in absolute value than E[p′/p –1|sell], giving buyer15s > seller15s.

Hence Shanghai reproduces the same SZSE signature: Yes.

Nasdaq is T+0 tradable, no daily price limits, free general short selling, symmetric taxes and institution/HFT dominated. HKEX allows genuine short selling, has no T+1 / daily price-limit regime, T+2 settlement and a much more institutional market-making ecology. In both buy and sell price impact is essentially symmetric and the classic bid-ask bounce / short-term reversal dominates the 15s horizon, so E[buyer15s] < E[seller15s] is recovered and the systematic buy > sell 15s premium disappears: No for Nasdaq, No for HKEX.

The asymmetric buyer/seller motivation highlighted in E – buys as conviction/accumulation urgency vs sells as liquidity/rebalancing – is the behavioural downstream of the T+1, price-limit, short-sale constrained, tax-asymmetric institution set. The proximate channel is therefore the mainland-specific special trading rules and regulations, i.e. the Yes, No, No; Special trading rules and regulations case O.

