I agree with the Yes, No, No empirical pattern and with the mainland regime diagnosis.

With buyer 15s = p′/p –1 and seller 15s = –(p′/p –1) where p′ is the 15s later mid, mechanical bid-ask bounce gives E[buyer15s]≈ –½ spread <0< +½ spread≈E[seller15s] under zero drift. A systematic E[buyer15s] > E[seller15s] as observed in SZSE normal trading therefore needs trade-sign drift on the 15s horizon large enough to overcome the ≈½ spread penalty for buys and the ≈½ spread benefit for sells.

That drift is a mainland A-share regime artefact.

SZSE and SSE share:
* T+1 delivery, no same-day reversal of a long position, so the seller set is mechanically pre-existing longs;
* ±10% daily price limits with a closing call auction;
* de-facto short-sale prohibition / severe short-sale constraints for the retail dominated universe;
* sell-side only 0.1% ad valorem stamp duty;
* retail dominated order flow.

With short selling essentially unavailable negative information cannot be expressed on the sell side. The supply side is mechanically constrained and a buy market order can lift the ask and create a persistent order-book imbalance that persists for tens of seconds because T+1, price limits and short-sale constraints blunt arbitrage. Sell-initiated flow is liquidity / distress / profit-taking dominated, its impact is muted and reverts faster. Hence E[p′/p –1|buy] can be >0 and |E[p′/p –1|buy]| > |E[p′/p –1|sell]| so E[buyer15s] > E[seller15s] on the mainland. Shanghai reproduces the same T+1 / price-limit / short-sale-constrained / tax-asymmetric regime as Shenzhen: Yes for SSE.

Nasdaq is T+0 tradable, no daily price limits, free general short selling, symmetric transaction taxes and institution/HFT dominated. HKEX allows genuine short selling, has no T+1 / daily price-limit regime, T+2 settlement and a much more institutional market-making ecology. In both markets buy and sell price impact is essentially symmetric and the classic bid-ask bounce / short-term reversal dominates the 15s horizon, i.e. E[buyer15s] < E[seller15s] is recovered and the systematic buy > sell 15s premium disappears: No for Nasdaq, No for HKEX.

Orderbook imbalance is the proximate 15s microstructure channel through which the rules operate, and asymmetric buyer/seller motivation is the behavioural downstream read-out of those constraints. In the option taxonomy the institutional root that differentiates mainland China from Nasdaq and HKEX is Special trading rules and regulations.

Option O – Yes for SSE, No for Nasdaq, No for HKEX; Special trading rules and regulations.

